# crav1 kit

Specs, not chat history, are the source of truth. Put change work under `docs/specs/<change-id>/`. The layout always includes `docs/system/`. Spark and ideas seed a thin landscape when that folder is missing, including `glossary.md`, and do not rewrite an existing one except new glossary rows and the picture update. They fill a missing `glossary.md` when the folder already exists, and append only new rows when the file exists. Intake writes the landscape, including `glossary.md`, and does not skip it. Existing glossary rows are not rewritten. The picture update adds what is new to the short description, the diagram, and how the parts connect, and does not rewrite what is already there. A meaning the source does not state is `to be researched`.

When the user asks for startup options, name only these seven: `/crav1-spark-to-spec`, `/crav1-ideas-to-spec`, `/crav1-intake-to-specs`, `/crav1-match-to-specs`, `/crav1-repos-to-spec`, `/crav1-environment-read`, and `/crav1-fix-bug`. Every other skill is a later skill. That ask names the list. It does not run a skill and does not move work into a lane. Each starter still follows the rules it already has. `/crav1-repos-to-spec` still runs only when the user names repos. `/crav1-environment-read` still runs only when the user names the host. Naming production, prod, or live stops. `/crav1-fix-bug` still runs only when the user names a real bug.

Type `/crav1` to list kit skills. Typical order: spark, ideas, or intake → feature branch (`feat/<slug>` or specify-only `spec/<slug>`) → architecture review → tighten → resolve questions → plan → review-plan → tighten-plan → implement (or `/crav1-complete-task` / `/crav1-complete-tasks` / `/crav1-complete-features`) → verify → fix-from-verify (do not edit `spec.md` in the fix loop). `/crav1-plan-from-spec` names whether this repo has a linter or checker for the code the tasks will touch. When it has one, the plan names it and Build is expected to leave that check green. When it does not, the plan says so and stops before Build. The user decides to add the linter or to go on without one. Do not install one from the plan skill. Specify does not name the linter. `/crav1-spark-to-spec` is greenfield, a brownfield feature, or a later feature on `docs/system/` (new slug unless they extend). It seeds a thin `docs/system/` when that folder is missing, including `glossary.md`, then the spec, then one index row. `/crav1-intake-to-specs` writes the landscape, including `glossary.md`, plus one spec per v0 feature. `/crav1-match-to-specs` matches existing repos plus a dump to the landscape, including `glossary.md`, and one spec per confirmed slice. It does not replace spark, ideas, or intake. `/crav1-match-dump-to-specs` sorts a later dump onto specs that already exist. It does not create a slug. It does not seed `docs/system/`. A missing `glossary.md` is filled only when that folder already exists, and new rows are appended when the file exists. `/crav1-code-into-specs` sorts a code change that already landed onto specs that already exist. No dump is required. It does not create a slug. It does not watch the repo. It does not seed `docs/system/`. A missing `glossary.md` is filled only when that folder already exists, and new rows are appended when the file exists. `/crav1-add-to-spec` adds new information to one existing spec and reports impact on the landscape or other specs before editing them. It does not create a glossary. A new glossary row can be one listed edit. A kept addition from `/crav1-meeting-to-specs` is handed here. The impact check still runs. `/crav1-security-review` is cross-cutting. It runs only when architecture already exists (`docs/system/` or a spec that already describes the design). The same command covers the whole system, one existing spec, or the change in front of us. It writes kept findings and stops. It does not plan, implement, or commit. Spark, plan, and verify do not run it. `/crav1-repos-to-spec` is cross-cutting. The user names one or more repos. It reads those repos and writes one architecture spec the lanes can extend at `docs/architecture/spec.md` when that file is missing. The folder is `docs/architecture/`. The file is `spec.md`. That path is locked. It stays out of `docs/system` and out of `docs/specs`. Do not invent any other folder. `docs/specs` stays one folder per feature slug. `docs/system` stays the short picture. When a real note is already in `docs/system/`, it does not rewrite those notes and writes the missing architecture spec and `docs/architecture/left-out.md`. When `docs/architecture/spec.md` is already there, a later re-read adds only what is new and does not rewrite lines that are already there. It also reads `docs/architecture/left-out.md` and removes a line when the code shows it. The same rule applies to system notes that are already there. When those notes are missing, it writes the `docs/system/` notes `/crav1-explain` reads. A fact the code shows is confirmed. Before any file is written, it confirms in sections, one question each: one per named repo, inferred guesses, links, and left-out. Every item stays listed. A guess stays out of the spec until that section accepts it, and then it is accepted from a guess, not a code fact. A guess that stays left out is written to `docs/architecture/left-out.md`. The left-out answer can accept a line, keep it left out, or dismiss it. Dismiss means it is not offered again. A link between repos is written only when the code shows it. If none is found, it says so. It does not invent links. Confirming a guess does not turn it into a link. It does not design the next feature, write application code, or start Specify, Plan, or Build. It is not `/crav1-code-into-specs`. `/crav1-environment-read` is cross-cutting. The user names the host at the start. It still runs only when the user names the host. Naming production, prod, or live stops. It does not guess the host. It never reads production data. One pass reads every non-prod environment. Each seen line keeps its own environment. Each host has its own reader. This build has readers for Azure, AWS, and Google Cloud. Any other host stops with no reader and does not pretend to read it. It does not read Azure DevOps, GitHub, or CI. The user names the host, not the subscription, the account, or the project. Marks come from the name, the group, the tags, and `docs/environments/marks.md` when that file exists. Pre-prod, a resource shared with prod, and a prod-only resource are shape and connections only, after one yes. Prod-only means the only mark is production. A no lists them as not read, and a declined prod-only resource stays skipped. A line from that yes says `prod only`. The skill appends only confirmed lines to `docs/environments/marks.md`. It does not write `docs/system`, `docs/architecture/spec.md`, a feature spec, or `docs/architecture/left-out.md`. A fact is seen in that environment, not something the code shows. It does not become a link unless the environment shows the connection. After the first read, and before any section, it can look inside a database or storage when the user says yes. SQL is metadata only. Storage is structure only. It never reads a row or a blob. Production databases and production storage are never opened. A prod-only shape read does not open them. A database with no clear environment is asked before it is opened. It does not install a tool, and it does not create a login, a firewall rule, a role, or a key. Confirmation is one section at a time. After the user confirms a section, the skill that already owns the file adds only what is new. It does not rewrite a line that is already there. It does not start Specify, Plan, or Build. `/crav1-pipeline-environments` is cross-cutting. The user names Azure DevOps, the org, and the project. A pipeline name is optional. If any of those three is missing, it stops and asks. Options only. It does not guess. It does not ask for a typed path. It reads pipeline definitions with `az pipelines` and `az devops` (list and show only). It does not run, queue, edit, approve, or trigger a pipeline. It does not read builds, logs, test runs, boards, or repo files beyond the pipeline definition. Secrets are not printed. The stage name gives the environment, with the same whole-word rule as `/crav1-environment-read`. A deploy step marks the resource it names. The group is context. The rest of the group is not marked. It appends only confirmed lines to `docs/environments/marks.md`. It creates that file only after at least one confirmed line. It never rewrites an existing line. It does not write `docs/system`, `docs/architecture/spec.md`, a feature spec, or `docs/architecture/left-out.md`. If `az` is missing, the `azure-devops` extension is missing, or there is no login, the stop names which. It does not install or log in. It is a later skill. It is not a starter option. When the user asks for startup options, that ask names the seven and does not run this command. The seven starter options stay unchanged. It does not start Specify, Plan, or Build. It names `/crav1-environment-read` and does not run it. `/crav1-explain` is cross-cutting. It reads `docs/system/` and the first answer is a short TLDR. Longer goes one level deeper from the same notes. The user can point it at one part. A question about whether the system can do something is yes with a pointer, no, or that it is not written down. It does not guess, write a second document, teach, or start Specify, Plan, or Build. When the picture is older than the specs, it says so and names `/crav1-keep-current`. It does not run it. `/crav1-keep-current` is cross-cutting. It adds what is new to the short description, the diagram, and how the parts connect. It does not rewrite what is already there. It does not design the change and does not build it. It can run on its own. Spark, ideas, intake, match, and any sibling that already appends a glossary row also run this picture update in that same pass. They do not rewrite existing glossary rows. `/crav1-review-pr` is cross-cutting. It runs only when the user names an open pull request in that turn, or clearly asks to review that pull request. It reads the diff and names the lane for a finding. It does not edit code, the spec, or the plan, and it does not vote, comment, install a linter, or merge. Spark, specify, and verify do not run it. `/crav1-open-pr` may name it and does not run it. `/crav1-merge-pr` does not run it. A review is not a merge ask. `/crav1-fix-bug` is cross-cutting. It runs only when the user names a real bug. If they have not named one, it stops. It does not go hunting. A real bug is a verify failure on work that is already shipping, or a defect that comes in from outside. A design gap found before Build stays in the spec. An unfinished task stays a task. A one-off flaky check is a note, unless it keeps blocking ship. It says where the bug was seen (verify, a report, or production), names the bug, points at the spec or the shipped behavior it breaks, and names the lane. A spec miss goes to Specify. A plan miss goes to Plan. A verify miss or a broken implementation goes to Build. It does not start that lane, edit code, open a pull request, or create an Azure Boards work item. It is not a lane and not a stretch of verify. When the user asks for startup options, it is one of those seven. That ask names the list and does not run it. It is an intake for a bug that already exists. It still runs only when the user names a real bug. It is not a skill that runs because a repo is new, and it is not started automatically. Spark, specify, plan, and verify do not run it. `/crav1-exploratory-test` is cross-cutting. It runs after a build of a slice already exists. Pass one learns the slice by using it once. Pass two is no longer careful. It adds randomness: sometimes a double-click, a hover back and forth, repeating an action, or using the same control more than once. It follows what the last action showed. It lasts about as long as the first pass, long enough to provoke bugs, then it stops. That stop is named `matched the first pass`. It stops sooner when the user says stop. That stop is named `the user said stop`. It does not run forever. The user does not set a clock. It writes bugs to `docs/specs/<slug>/explore.md`. It does not write the checks the plan already named. It does not start Specify, Plan, or Build. It does not move work into a lane. A bug it finds is not sent to `/crav1-fix-bug` unless the user says so in that turn. It may run from any lane. It is a later skill. It is not a starter option. When the user asks for startup options, name only those seven. That ask names the list and does not run this command. `/crav1-suggest-tests-for-code` is cross-cutting. The user points at code that already exists, one repo or one area, not the whole system. It reads that code and suggests tests for what that code can actually break. A function gets a few checks. A page gets a browser check. A boundary gets an integration check. It does not dump every test type. Every suggestion stays listed. Nothing is dropped to keep the list short. The user takes them one at a time: keep, leave, or dismiss. Dismiss means it is not offered again. A kept suggestion goes onto the existing spec through `/crav1-add-to-spec`. Plan turns it into a task. It does not write the tests, change the code, or start Specify, Plan, or Build. It does not move work into a lane. It may run from any lane. It is a later skill. It is not a starter option. When the user asks for startup options, name only those seven. That ask names the list and does not run this command. The seven starter options stay unchanged. `/crav1-meeting-to-specs` is cross-cutting. Everything after the command, and every `@`, is the minutes or transcript. If there is no pointer, it stops and asks. Options only. It does not guess. It extracts requirements, decisions, changes, open questions, and bugs. Each item is a quote. It includes the speaker and the time when the transcript shows them. It drops small talk and status chatter. It matches each item against existing `docs/specs/<slug>/` folders and skips `_template`. The cases are an addition to a named existing spec, a possible new feature, a bug, or unclear. When more than one existing spec could fit, it lists every candidate. It does not drop an item to keep the list short. The user takes them one at a time: keep, leave, or dismiss. Dismiss is for this run only. The next run starts fresh. It does not write a dismissals file. A kept addition goes to that spec through `/crav1-add-to-spec`, with a source label from the meeting and the date when it is known. The impact check still runs there. A kept new feature names `/crav1-spark-to-spec`, `/crav1-ideas-to-spec`, or `/crav1-intake-to-specs` and does not run it. A kept bug names `/crav1-fix-bug` and does not run it. It writes nothing itself. It does not copy the transcript into the repo. It does not start Specify, Plan, or Build. It does not move work into a lane. It may run from any lane. It is a later skill. It is not a starter option. When the user asks for startup options, name only those seven. That ask names the list and does not run this command. The seven starter options stay unchanged. `/crav1-whats-known-about` is cross-cutting. The user asks about one feature, slice, resource, or not-yet-feature. The answer comes from the specs first. It reads only. It writes nothing. An exact name match runs first, across spec titles, `tasks.md`, Match quotes, `docs/system/`, `docs/architecture/spec.md`, and `docs/environments/marks.md`. A sentence uses the key words, with spec titles and Match quotes weighted highest. One pass. It does not hunt the whole codebase. One slice that wins is the answer, and the path is named. Two or more close hits are listed, one line each, and it asks which. It does not guess. No slice: it says so, shows what the repo, the code, and the environment notes know, and presents a candidate slice. It names `/crav1-spark-to-spec`, `/crav1-ideas-to-spec`, or `/crav1-intake-to-specs` and does not run them. Pasted minutes or a long dump names `/crav1-meeting-to-specs` or `/crav1-match-dump-to-specs` and stops. After the repo part it runs read-only live checks: Azure resource settings and recent changes with `az`, and Azure DevOps work items, open pull requests, and branches that touch the thing. Each live fact is tagged as a live read. Production follows the `/crav1-environment-read` rule: shape and connections only, after the same yes or no. It never reads data. A failed live read shows one prompt with the issue, a fix when there is one, and Retry or Skip. Skip is the repo-only answer, with a line that the live checks did not run and why. A focus still runs the full read and shows only those sections. It may name one skill. It does not run another skill. It does not start Specify, Plan, or Build. It is a later skill. It is not a starter option. When the user asks for startup options, name only those seven. That ask names the list and does not run this command. The seven starter options stay unchanged. It is not `/crav1-explain`. Explain reads `docs/system/` and does not open a spec to answer the question.

Do not scaffold an application unless the user asked for one. Prefer editing the spec or plan when implementation drifts.

Full install paths: `docs/install.md` in the kit repo. After a plugin install, start with the plugin README.
